1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:04,589
At the Corner Cafe, Asha keeps a list of
today's customers.

2
00:00:04,609 --> 00:00:06,067
Omar loves the cafe.

3
00:00:06,087 --> 00:00:08,210
He is in the list four times.

4
00:00:08,233 --> 00:00:10,360
So len counts visits, not people.

5
00:00:10,380 --> 00:00:13,158
How many different customers came in
today?

6
00:00:13,178 --> 00:00:14,654
A list cannot tell her.

7
00:00:15,253 --> 00:00:20,813
This is Code Quest, Chapter 13: tuples,
for fixed groups, and sets, which keep

8
00:00:20,833 --> 00:00:22,243
each item only once.

9
00:00:22,267 --> 00:00:27,834
And an AI assistant will sound completely
sure about empty brackets, and be wrong.

10
00:00:27,854 --> 00:00:29,061
You will catch it.

11
00:00:29,653 --> 00:00:31,488
So here is today's mission.

12
00:00:31,508 --> 00:00:35,333
Line 2 is the list of today's visits, with
every repeat.

13
00:00:35,367 --> 00:00:39,792
Line 5 makes a set from it, so each
customer counts once.

14
00:00:39,812 --> 00:00:43,457
Line 4 keeps the opening hours as a fixed
pair.

15
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:46,838
And lines 9 to 11 compare today with
yesterday.

16
00:00:46,858 --> 00:00:47,910
Let's build it.

17
00:00:48,483 --> 00:00:53,741
Write and unpack tuples, remove repeats
with a set, compare two sets, and choose

18
00:00:53,761 --> 00:00:57,567
the right collection with an AI that
compares the options.

19
00:00:58,150 --> 00:01:01,366
A set keeps each item once, so it counts
people.

20
00:01:01,386 --> 00:01:05,074
A tuple keeps a fixed group, such as the
opening hours.

21
00:01:05,094 --> 00:01:10,669
Booking sites and maps use both, and the
right choice makes code shorter and safer.

22
00:01:11,250 --> 00:01:13,151
You now know four collections.

23
00:01:13,171 --> 00:01:17,760
A list, in square brackets, keeps order,
allows repeats, and can change.

24
00:01:17,800 --> 00:01:21,760
A tuple, in round brackets, is like a list
that cannot change.

25
00:01:21,800 --> 00:01:25,005
A set uses curly brackets with values
inside.

26
00:01:25,025 --> 00:01:28,660
It keeps no order, and each item appears
only once.

27
00:01:28,700 --> 00:01:33,525
And a dictionary also uses curly brackets,
but with keys and values.

28
00:01:33,545 --> 00:01:35,092
Each key appears once.

29
00:01:35,687 --> 00:01:39,991
Think of a set as a bag of name cards, in
no fixed order.

30
00:01:40,011 --> 00:01:44,771
Unlike a real bag, a set ignores a card
that is already inside.

31
00:01:44,791 --> 00:01:48,489
Add Omar four times, and it still holds
one Omar.

32
00:01:49,087 --> 00:01:49,859
Quick check.

33
00:01:49,879 --> 00:01:53,952
Which collection keeps each item only
once, in no fixed order?

34
00:02:05,313 --> 00:02:05,464
D.

35
00:02:05,484 --> 00:02:05,976
A set.

36
00:02:05,996 --> 00:02:08,960
Each item once, and no fixed order.

37
00:02:09,553 --> 00:02:10,621
Take a breather.

38
00:02:10,641 --> 00:02:12,593
Here is what you know so far.

39
00:02:12,633 --> 00:02:16,038
Next, Asha stores the opening hours as a
tuple.

40
00:02:20,783 --> 00:02:21,285
Step 1.

41
00:02:21,305 --> 00:02:24,265
Asha's cafe opens at 8 and closes at 18.

42
00:02:24,285 --> 00:02:29,479
Line 2 keeps both numbers in a tuple, with
round brackets and a comma.

43
00:02:29,499 --> 00:02:31,863
The comma is what makes a tuple.

44
00:02:31,900 --> 00:02:35,560
Line 3 keeps one special: a scone and its
price.

45
00:02:35,580 --> 00:02:38,090
A tuple can hold different types.

46
00:02:38,133 --> 00:02:41,983
You read a tuple with an index, exactly
like a list.

47
00:02:42,003 --> 00:02:43,992
Index 0 is the first value.

48
00:02:55,347 --> 00:02:59,827
Line 4 prints the whole tuple, 8 and 18,
in round brackets.

49
00:02:59,867 --> 00:03:03,874
Then lines 5 and 6 print 8 and 18, and
Scone.

50
00:03:03,894 --> 00:03:10,407
Index 0 of hours is 8, index 1 is 18, and
index 0 of special is the name.

51
00:03:10,433 --> 00:03:15,214
Line 7 counts 2 values, and line 8 shows
the type: tuple.

52
00:03:15,817 --> 00:03:16,271
Step 2.

53
00:03:16,291 --> 00:03:21,154
Unpacking puts each value of a tuple into
its own variable, in one line.

54
00:03:21,174 --> 00:03:25,087
Line 3 gives opens the first value, and
closes the second.

55
00:03:25,133 --> 00:03:30,051
The number of names on the left must match
the number of values.

56
00:03:30,071 --> 00:03:34,063
Line 5 unpacks the scone and its price the
same way.

57
00:03:34,100 --> 00:03:35,177
Now lines 7 to 9.

58
00:03:35,197 --> 00:03:37,114
First is Omar, second is Lina.

59
00:03:37,134 --> 00:03:42,148
Line 9 builds a tuple on the right from
the old values, second then first, and

60
00:03:42,168 --> 00:03:43,181
then unpacks it.

61
00:03:54,547 --> 00:03:55,700
Lina, then Omar.

62
00:03:55,720 --> 00:03:59,587
The two names swapped places, with no
third variable.

63
00:03:59,633 --> 00:04:02,440
Remember the swap question from Chapter 2?

64
00:04:02,460 --> 00:04:04,123
This is the short answer.

65
00:04:04,143 --> 00:04:07,353
And lines 4 and 6 print the hours and the
scone.

66
00:04:07,950 --> 00:04:08,418
Step 3.

67
00:04:08,438 --> 00:04:11,832
A list of tuples is a common way to keep
records.

68
00:04:11,852 --> 00:04:14,270
Each special is a name and a price.

69
00:04:14,300 --> 00:04:20,098
Line 6 unpacks each tuple into name and
price, the same way items did with a

70
00:04:20,118 --> 00:04:22,012
dictionary in Chapter 12.

71
00:04:22,032 --> 00:04:25,610
Lines 7 and 8 print the line and add the
price.

72
00:04:25,633 --> 00:04:29,853
Line 10 reads in two steps, like the
nested menu in Chapter 12.

73
00:04:29,873 --> 00:04:32,208
Specials, index 1, gives one tuple.

74
00:04:32,228 --> 00:04:34,563
Then index 0 reads from that tuple.

75
00:04:45,913 --> 00:04:46,468
Scone.

76
00:04:46,488 --> 00:04:53,073
Index 1 is the second tuple, scone and
2.75, and index 0 is its name.

77
00:04:53,100 --> 00:04:58,828
And the loop printed each special, lined
up, with a total of 10.50.

78
00:04:59,420 --> 00:05:00,048
Breather.

79
00:05:00,068 --> 00:05:01,200
Tuples are done.

80
00:05:01,220 --> 00:05:04,079
Next, the hero of this chapter: the set.

81
00:05:08,817 --> 00:05:09,302
Step 4.

82
00:05:09,322 --> 00:05:12,769
Line 2 lists five visits, with Omar three
times.

83
00:05:12,789 --> 00:05:17,247
Line 3 turns the list into a set, and
every repeat disappears.

84
00:05:17,267 --> 00:05:22,531
A set has no order, so it has no
positions, and printed directly, its names

85
00:05:22,551 --> 00:05:24,644
can change order between runs.

86
00:05:24,664 --> 00:05:28,097
So line 6 prints sorted, a list in a fixed
order.

87
00:05:28,133 --> 00:05:33,030
A set compares the exact text, so Omar and
omar would be two different items.

88
00:05:33,050 --> 00:05:37,308
And in checks a set quickly, even when it
holds thousands of names.

89
00:05:48,680 --> 00:05:49,345
5, then 3.

90
00:05:49,365 --> 00:05:54,000
The list has five visits, but the set has
three different customers.

91
00:05:54,033 --> 00:05:57,776
Line 6 shows them in order: Lina, Omar,
Sam.

92
00:05:57,796 --> 00:06:00,854
Lina is in the set, and Ravi is not.

93
00:06:01,450 --> 00:06:02,006
Step 5.

94
00:06:02,026 --> 00:06:04,147
Line 2 makes an empty set.

95
00:06:04,167 --> 00:06:06,205
Add puts an item into it.

96
00:06:06,225 --> 00:06:09,828
Lines 3 to 5 add Omar, Lina, and Omar
again.

97
00:06:09,848 --> 00:06:12,380
Remember the bag of name cards.

98
00:06:12,400 --> 00:06:16,491
Line 6 prints the sorted names and the
number of items.

99
00:06:27,847 --> 00:06:29,382
Lina and Omar, and 2.

100
00:06:29,402 --> 00:06:33,457
The second Omar changed nothing: he was
already inside.

101
00:06:33,500 --> 00:06:35,303
Discard takes an item out.

102
00:06:35,323 --> 00:06:39,930
Line 8 discards Zoe, who is not in the
set, and there is no error.

103
00:06:39,967 --> 00:06:42,373
And line 10 shows an important rule.

104
00:06:42,393 --> 00:06:45,473
Empty curly brackets make an empty
dictionary.

105
00:06:45,493 --> 00:06:49,046
To make an empty set, write set, with
round brackets.

106
00:06:49,653 --> 00:06:50,351
Quick check.

107
00:06:50,371 --> 00:06:52,086
How do you make an empty set?

108
00:07:03,447 --> 00:07:03,572
B.

109
00:07:03,592 --> 00:07:05,393
Set, with round brackets.

110
00:07:05,413 --> 00:07:08,233
Empty curly brackets make a dictionary.

111
00:07:08,253 --> 00:07:09,836
Keep this one in mind.

112
00:07:10,417 --> 00:07:10,953
Step 6.

113
00:07:10,973 --> 00:07:13,494
Four operators compare two sets.

114
00:07:13,514 --> 00:07:17,227
The vertical bar is union: items in either
set.

115
00:07:17,267 --> 00:07:20,667
The and sign is intersection: items in
both.

116
00:07:20,700 --> 00:07:24,520
Minus is difference: items in the first
set only.

117
00:07:24,567 --> 00:07:29,520
And the hat sign, called a caret, is
symmetric difference: items in one set,

118
00:07:29,540 --> 00:07:30,371
but not both.

119
00:07:30,987 --> 00:07:33,851
Today, Omar, Lina and Sam came in.

120
00:07:33,871 --> 00:07:36,397
Yesterday, Lina, Ravi and Sam.

121
00:07:36,433 --> 00:07:39,516
Line 6 takes yesterday away from today.

122
00:07:50,880 --> 00:07:51,675
Omar, only.

123
00:07:51,695 --> 00:07:54,120
He came today, but not yesterday.

124
00:07:54,167 --> 00:07:59,204
Union gives all four names, and
intersection gives Lina and Sam, who came

125
00:07:59,224 --> 00:07:59,897
both days.

126
00:07:59,933 --> 00:08:04,095
The caret gives Omar and Ravi: each came
on one day only.

127
00:08:04,115 --> 00:08:08,716
And for minus, order matters: yesterday
minus today gives Ravi.

128
00:08:09,317 --> 00:08:10,685
Back to the mission.

129
00:08:10,705 --> 00:08:13,949
Line 2 lists today's visits, with every
repeat.

130
00:08:13,969 --> 00:08:16,657
Line 3 is yesterday's set of customers.

131
00:08:16,700 --> 00:08:22,549
Line 4 unpacks the opening hours, line 5
makes the set of today's customers, and

132
00:08:22,569 --> 00:08:24,310
line 6 prints the hours.

133
00:08:24,333 --> 00:08:29,933
Lines 9 to 11 compare the two days, and
line 12 checks for Omar with in.

134
00:08:29,967 --> 00:08:33,700
And line 7 counts the list, while line 8
counts the set.

135
00:08:45,080 --> 00:08:46,103
Visits today, 7.

136
00:08:46,123 --> 00:08:47,602
Different customers, 3.

137
00:08:47,622 --> 00:08:50,860
The list counts visits, and the set counts
people.

138
00:08:50,900 --> 00:08:52,921
Lina and Sam came both days.

139
00:08:52,941 --> 00:08:55,910
Omar is new today, and Ravi missed today.

140
00:08:55,933 --> 00:08:59,933
And line 12 checks the set with in: Omar
came today, True.

141
00:08:59,967 --> 00:09:02,048
Each collection does one job.

142
00:09:02,068 --> 00:09:03,280
Mission complete!

143
00:09:03,300 --> 00:09:09,150
Next, Asha welcomes each new customer,
with an AI assistant, one stage at a time.

144
00:09:09,750 --> 00:09:14,637
This chapter's AI skill is asking for
trade-offs: what you gain and what you

145
00:09:14,657 --> 00:09:16,057
lose with each choice.

146
00:09:16,077 --> 00:09:21,223
Ask for a strength and a weakness for each
option, and you get an answer you can

147
00:09:21,243 --> 00:09:21,610
check.

148
00:09:22,220 --> 00:09:23,141
Stage 1: plan.

149
00:09:23,161 --> 00:09:28,656
Asha wants to welcome each customer the
first time they come in, in the order they

150
00:09:28,676 --> 00:09:29,126
arrive.

151
00:09:29,146 --> 00:09:34,170
She asks the AI to compare all four
collections, and not to choose for her.

152
00:09:34,200 --> 00:09:37,372
The AI gives a strength and a weakness for
each.

153
00:09:37,392 --> 00:09:42,492
Asha chooses: the visit list keeps the
order, and a set remembers who she has

154
00:09:42,512 --> 00:09:43,622
already welcomed.

155
00:09:44,220 --> 00:09:45,329
Stage 2: write.

156
00:09:45,349 --> 00:09:51,574
Asha writes the first lines herself, and
asks the AI to explain line 3 with her own

157
00:09:51,594 --> 00:09:51,950
list.

158
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:57,842
The AI explains: set of today keeps each
name once, so the length is 3, and Sam is

159
00:09:57,862 --> 00:09:58,271
in it.

160
00:09:58,291 --> 00:10:01,416
She runs it, and the worked example
matches.

161
00:10:02,020 --> 00:10:03,281
Stage 3: generate.

162
00:10:03,301 --> 00:10:08,692
Asha asks for the welcome code, with an
empty set called seen, and the exact

163
00:10:08,712 --> 00:10:09,190
output.

164
00:10:09,233 --> 00:10:13,610
The AI promises three welcomes, then New
today, 3.

165
00:10:13,630 --> 00:10:14,929
Look at line 3.

166
00:10:26,280 --> 00:10:26,479
No.

167
00:10:26,499 --> 00:10:31,380
Remember step 5: empty curly brackets make
a dictionary, not a set.

168
00:10:31,987 --> 00:10:32,972
Asha runs it.

169
00:10:32,992 --> 00:10:37,227
One welcome for Omar, then the program
stops on line 7.

170
00:10:37,267 --> 00:10:41,371
No attribute add means a dictionary has no
add method.

171
00:10:41,391 --> 00:10:44,273
The AI sounded sure, and it was wrong.

172
00:10:44,293 --> 00:10:47,252
Set, with round brackets, fixes line 3.

173
00:10:47,853 --> 00:10:48,961
Stage 4: test.

174
00:10:48,981 --> 00:10:55,405
Asha fixes line 3, and asks for three test
cases with the exact expected output.

175
00:10:55,425 --> 00:10:58,063
Her rule: one welcome per person.

176
00:10:58,100 --> 00:11:00,038
The AI predicts each case.

177
00:11:00,058 --> 00:11:05,535
For case 3, Omar and omar with a space, it
predicts one welcome for Omar.

178
00:11:16,913 --> 00:11:17,105
No.

179
00:11:17,125 --> 00:11:19,224
A set compares the exact text.

180
00:11:19,244 --> 00:11:24,168
Omar with a capital O, and omar with a
space, are two different names.

181
00:11:24,787 --> 00:11:26,352
Asha runs every case.

182
00:11:26,372 --> 00:11:30,956
The normal day and the empty day print
what the AI predicted.

183
00:11:30,976 --> 00:11:36,692
But case 3 welcomes Omar twice: once as
Omar, and once as omar with a space.

184
00:11:37,287 --> 00:11:40,669
Case 3 fails her rule: one welcome per
person.

185
00:11:40,689 --> 00:11:44,071
And the AI's prediction did not match the
run.

186
00:11:44,091 --> 00:11:46,142
Only running it showed both.

187
00:11:46,753 --> 00:11:47,839
Stage 5: debug.

188
00:11:47,859 --> 00:11:52,927
Asha cleans each name on line 5, with
strip and title from Chapter 3.

189
00:11:52,947 --> 00:11:56,835
But she types line 8 too fast, and the
program stops.

190
00:11:56,855 --> 00:12:01,922
She pastes the code and the full error,
and asks for the cause first.

191
00:12:02,520 --> 00:12:05,851
The AI names line 8: square brackets after
add.

192
00:12:05,871 --> 00:12:08,988
Add is a method, so it needs round
brackets.

193
00:12:09,008 --> 00:12:13,337
Not subscriptable means you cannot use
square brackets on it.

194
00:12:13,953 --> 00:12:18,636
A fix can break a case that worked before,
so Asha runs all three cases again.

195
00:12:18,656 --> 00:12:22,073
Lines 2 to 4 keep each case name with its
list of visits.

196
00:12:22,100 --> 00:12:26,440
Line 9 cleans each name, and line 12 now
uses round brackets.

197
00:12:26,467 --> 00:12:29,814
The normal day: three welcomes, New today
3.

198
00:12:29,834 --> 00:12:30,197
Pass.

199
00:12:30,233 --> 00:12:34,192
Omar typed two ways: one welcome for Omar,
then Lina.

200
00:12:34,212 --> 00:12:35,168
New today, 2.

201
00:12:35,188 --> 00:12:35,543
Pass.

202
00:12:35,567 --> 00:12:38,150
And the empty day: New today, 0.

203
00:12:38,170 --> 00:12:38,557
Pass.

204
00:12:38,577 --> 00:12:40,266
All three cases pass.

205
00:12:40,887 --> 00:12:42,163
Stage 6: improve.

206
00:12:42,183 --> 00:12:46,203
Asha asks for one change that makes the
code shorter.

207
00:12:46,223 --> 00:12:50,167
And she adds: the output must stay exactly
the same.

208
00:12:50,200 --> 00:12:54,363
The AI suggests making the set from the
whole list on line 3.

209
00:12:54,383 --> 00:12:57,175
Then the add line is not needed, it says.

210
00:12:57,787 --> 00:13:00,084
Asha tests it on the normal day.

211
00:13:00,104 --> 00:13:04,718
Line 3 now fills the set with every name
before the loop starts.

212
00:13:04,738 --> 00:13:07,687
Line 6 checks if the name is not in seen.

213
00:13:19,047 --> 00:13:19,246
No.

214
00:13:19,266 --> 00:13:24,212
Every name is already in seen, so the
check on line 6 is never true.

215
00:13:24,232 --> 00:13:26,987
No welcomes at all, only New today, 3.

216
00:13:27,033 --> 00:13:31,201
The AI broke her rule: the output did not
stay the same.

217
00:13:31,221 --> 00:13:32,920
Asha keeps her version.

218
00:13:33,520 --> 00:13:34,225
Quick check.

219
00:13:34,245 --> 00:13:37,788
Which prompt gets an answer you can check
against your job?

220
00:13:49,147 --> 00:13:49,259
A.

221
00:13:49,279 --> 00:13:53,084
Ask for a comparison, with a strength and
a weakness each.

222
00:13:53,104 --> 00:13:54,140
Then you choose.

223
00:13:54,753 --> 00:13:55,369
Breather.

224
00:13:55,389 --> 00:13:58,262
Asha compared four options and chose one.

225
00:13:58,282 --> 00:14:04,050
Then she caught three AI mistakes: empty
curly brackets, a wrong prediction, and a

226
00:14:04,070 --> 00:14:05,955
change that broke her rule.

227
00:14:05,975 --> 00:14:09,837
Next, the mistakes beginners make with
tuples and sets.

228
00:14:14,583 --> 00:14:15,349
Mistake 1.

229
00:14:15,369 --> 00:14:17,236
Line 3 prints the hours.

230
00:14:17,256 --> 00:14:20,773
Line 4 tries to change the opening hour to
9.

231
00:14:20,800 --> 00:14:24,712
A tuple cannot change, so Python stops
with a Type Error.

232
00:14:24,732 --> 00:14:30,230
Does not support item assignment means you
cannot give one position a new value.

233
00:14:30,267 --> 00:14:33,550
The fix: make a new tuple, nine and
eighteen.

234
00:14:33,570 --> 00:14:36,560
Or use a list if the values change often.

235
00:14:37,153 --> 00:14:38,049
Now you try.

236
00:14:38,069 --> 00:14:41,713
Line 2 puts round brackets around one
word, tea.

237
00:14:41,733 --> 00:14:44,460
Remember step 1: what makes a tuple?

238
00:14:55,813 --> 00:14:57,314
A string, not a tuple.

239
00:14:57,334 --> 00:15:00,492
There is no comma, so the brackets do
nothing.

240
00:15:00,512 --> 00:15:01,943
Len counts 3 letters.

241
00:15:01,967 --> 00:15:05,988
There is no error, which makes this
mistake hard to see.

242
00:15:06,008 --> 00:15:09,381
The fix: a comma after the value, as on
line 4.

243
00:15:09,987 --> 00:15:10,734
One more.

244
00:15:10,754 --> 00:15:14,571
Line 4 asks the set of customers for index
0.

245
00:15:14,591 --> 00:15:15,936
Remember step 4.

246
00:15:27,313 --> 00:15:29,822
The program stops with a Type Error.

247
00:15:29,842 --> 00:15:32,843
A set has no order, so it has no
positions.

248
00:15:32,863 --> 00:15:35,863
Not subscriptable means no square
brackets.

249
00:15:35,900 --> 00:15:40,832
The fix: check a name with in, or use
sorted to get a list you can index.

250
00:15:41,453 --> 00:15:42,367
Last breather.

251
00:15:42,387 --> 00:15:46,766
Now you know the tuple and set mistakes
that catch most beginners.

252
00:15:51,517 --> 00:15:51,891
First.

253
00:15:51,911 --> 00:15:56,629
Asha has a set of customers who ordered
tea, and a set who ordered cake.

254
00:15:56,649 --> 00:16:00,051
Which operator gives the customers who
ordered both?

255
00:16:00,071 --> 00:16:02,288
And which gives tea, but not cake?

256
00:16:13,647 --> 00:16:16,197
Write both after the video, and run them.

257
00:16:16,787 --> 00:16:17,234
Second.

258
00:16:17,254 --> 00:16:19,570
A dictionary key must never change.

259
00:16:19,590 --> 00:16:24,176
Could a tuple, such as table 4 by the
window, be a key for a booking?

260
00:16:24,196 --> 00:16:25,044
Could a list?

261
00:16:36,413 --> 00:16:39,728
Try both, and read the error message if
you get one.

262
00:16:40,317 --> 00:16:41,105
Let's recap.

263
00:16:41,125 --> 00:16:46,361
A tuple is a fixed group you cannot
change, and unpacking puts each value into

264
00:16:46,381 --> 00:16:47,507
its own variable.

265
00:16:47,533 --> 00:16:50,484
A set keeps each item once, with no order.

266
00:16:50,504 --> 00:16:53,383
An empty set is set, with round brackets.

267
00:16:53,433 --> 00:16:57,873
Four operators compare two sets, and
sorted gives a fixed order.

268
00:16:57,900 --> 00:17:02,185
And with AI: ask for trade-offs, then
choose and test yourself.

269
00:17:02,787 --> 00:17:03,850
One last check.

270
00:17:03,870 --> 00:17:05,656
Set a holds tea and cake.

271
00:17:05,676 --> 00:17:07,607
Set b holds cake and scone.

272
00:17:07,627 --> 00:17:11,364
What does sorted of a and b give, with the
and sign?

273
00:17:22,747 --> 00:17:22,879
C.

274
00:17:22,899 --> 00:17:23,720
Cake, only.

275
00:17:23,740 --> 00:17:26,929
The and sign gives the items in both sets.

276
00:17:27,520 --> 00:17:28,806
Mission complete!

277
00:17:28,826 --> 00:17:32,034
You earned 100 XP and the Collector badge.

278
00:17:34,283 --> 00:17:38,226
Now it is your turn: eight tasks, worth
170 XP.

279
00:17:38,246 --> 00:17:44,887
Three warm-up tasks at 10 XP, and three
apply tasks at 20, including an allergy

280
00:17:44,907 --> 00:17:45,393
check.

281
00:17:45,433 --> 00:17:51,178
And two stretch tasks, worth 40 each: a
welcome with a reward, and an AI's set

282
00:17:51,198 --> 00:17:52,064
code to fix.

283
00:17:52,084 --> 00:17:55,168
Try each one before you open the solution.

284
00:17:55,750 --> 00:18:00,893
To make a list of every price with tax
added, Asha writes four lines: an empty

285
00:18:00,913 --> 00:18:03,540
list, a loop, the new price, and append.

286
00:18:03,567 --> 00:18:08,826
In Chapter 14, you will write that in one
line, with a comprehension, and learn when

287
00:18:08,846 --> 00:18:10,397
a normal loop is clearer.

288
00:18:10,433 --> 00:18:12,088
Do the warm-up tasks now.

289
00:18:12,108 --> 00:18:13,963
They take about ten minutes.

290
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:15,887
See you in Chapter 14.
